  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Thomas Curley named Highwood because the land was the highest ground between Chicago and Milwaukee and was covered with trees.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highwood%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Originally, the land was owned by the Potawatomi until it was ceded to the United States through the 1833 Treaty of Chicago.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highwood%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1851 Highwood became the headquarters of the Chicago North Shore and Milwaukee Railroad, now Metra's Union Pacific North Line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highwood%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "This eventually provoked federal authorities to enact the 'Highwood Quadrangle' which prohibited the sale of liquor within 1 1/8th miles of the army base.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highwood%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The city frequently hosts festivals for residents known as Celebrate Highwood which is hosted by the city chambers.",
